Note: user-defined words can override built-ins — since we check `token in words` before the built-ins, this works.

The "define a word that uses a word of the same name" test: `: foo 10 ;` `: foo foo 1 + ;` `foo` → execute body `["foo", "1", "+"]` → foo is in words → execute `["10"]` → push 10, then 1, + → 11. ✓

"Use different words with the same name": bar's body is `["foo"]`, foo is redefined to 6. bar → foo → 6? Wait, the test expects [5, 6]: `: foo 5 ;` `: bar foo ;` `: foo 6 ;` `bar foo` → bar → foo → 6, then foo → 6? No wait, expected is [5, 6]. Hmm: bar's body is `["foo"]`, and at execution time foo is the new definition (6). So bar → 6, foo → 6 → [6, 6]? But the test expects [5, 6].

Hmm, let me re-check the canonical data. Actually, in the canonical Forth exercise, this test is:

```json
{
  "uuid": "84b5d54f-6d6e-4a4f-8d4a-4d2d1e4b1e1e",
  "description": "user-defined words can use different words with the same name",
  "input": [": foo 5 ;", ": bar foo ;", ": foo 6 ;", "bar foo"],
  "expect": {"result": [5, 6]}
}
```

Hmm, this is the classic ambiguity. In real Forth, when defining `bar` as `foo`, the definition is compiled — at compile time, the word `foo` is resolved to its current definition. So bar contains the old foo (which pushes 5). This is the "immediate" vs "postponed" distinction. In the canonical solution, the definition body is compiled at definition time: when defining a word, each word in the body is resolved to its current definition (as a list of tokens to execute).

So the correct approach: at definition time, compile the body — for each token in the body, if it's a known word, replace it with that word's compiled body (or a marker), otherwise keep it as a token. Then bar's body = foo's compiled body at that point = [5]. Later redefining foo doesn't affect bar.

But then the "define a word that uses a word of the same name" test: `: foo 10 ;` `: foo foo 1 + ;` `foo` → 11. At the time of the second definition, `foo` in the body is resolved to the old foo's body [10]. So the new foo = [10, 1, +] → 11. ✓ This works with compile-time resolution.

And "can override user-defined words": `: foo dup ;` `: foo dup dup ;` `1 foo` → [1,1,1]. The second definition is compiled with the built-in dup. ✓

And "can override built-in words": `: swap dup ;` `1 swap` → [1,1]. ✓

And "can override built-in operators": `: + * ;` `3 4 +` → 12. ✓

So compile-time resolution it is. Implementation: the compiled body is a list of items, where each item is either a token string (number or built-in) or a nested list (a resolved word). Or more simply: at definition time, expand user-defined words into their bodies (flattening). But flattening at definition time breaks the "same name" test: `: foo foo 1 + ;` — at definition time, `foo` expands to the old foo's body [10] → new foo = [10, 1, +] → 11. ✓ This works too, with flattening.

But wait — flattening at definition time: `: bar foo ;` with foo = [5] → bar = [5]. Then `: foo 6 ;` → foo = [6]. `bar foo` → [5, 6]. ✓

Flattening is the simplest. But is there a test that requires lazy resolution? Let me check "can override user-defined words": `: foo dup ;` `: foo dup dup ;` `1 foo` — foo is redefined, and the new definition is used. ✓ With flattening.

Are there any tests where a word defined later is used inside an earlier definition? Scanning the tests... no. The canonical data doesn't have that. So flattening works and is simplest.

Actually, nested (non-flattened) bodies would also work and be more faithful. Either way. Let's go with flattening — simpler.

Hmm, one more consideration: numbers in the definition body stay as tokens and get pushed at execution time. No problem.

Let me also double-check the case-insensitivity of definitions: `: SWAP DUP Dup dup ;` `1 swap` → [1,1,1,1]. The name is lowercased: "swap". Body tokens are lowercased. Execution: `1 swap` → swap is in words → body [dup, dup, dup] → [1,1,1,1]. ✓ And since words are checked before built-ins, the user-defined swap overrides the built-in. ✓

Also "user-defined words are case-insensitive": `: foo dup ;` `1 FOO Foo foo` → each pushes a dup → [1,1,1,1]. ✓

Now, the `;` after a definition — my loop consumes it. A stray `;` at the top level: ignored (elif token == ";": pass). No problem.

Edge case: `: 1 2 ;` — name "1" is a number → ValueError("illegal operation"). ✓

Also, a definition without a terminating `;`? Not in the tests. My loop just consumes to the end. No problem.

